{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/204003-playing-as-cryptids-a-beginner\u2019s-guide","id":204003,"title":"Playing as Cryptids: A Beginner\u2019s Guide","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Demon: The Descent"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-07-21T11:34:31-07:00","id":204003,"title":"Playing as Cryptids: A Beginner\u2019s Guide","content":"<span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">I bet playing as a Cryptid could be a lot of fun. They\u2019re a weird, interesting part of the Demon setting. Here\u2019s how I\u2019d do it:</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Step 1: Choose a concept</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Since Cryptids are unique, and the experience of any given one will vary wildly from the normal assumptions of Demon, figuring out your concept is important.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">First of all, I don\u2019t know how a game where every member of the troupe plays a Cryptid would go down. Maybe it could be cool, maybe it would be a bit too outr\u00e9 and end up running in a completely different direction to Demon\u2019s themes. I see this working best in a chronicle that takes cues from </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Mummy: The Curse</span></i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">, with a structure in which some, but not necessarily all, players are demons, with others in the roles of Stigmatics, demon-blooded, and, of course, Cryptids.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Anyway, back to my original point, one\u2019s concept for a Cryptid is important. You should think about what species of animal you are, and how that reflects in the setting. Large or exotic animals are going to have a harder time going about unmolested, though smaller creatures might constantly live in fear of being trampled or eaten. Such is the life of a non-human living in an anthropocentic society. Of course, don\u2019t feel like you have to be restricted solely to animals native to your setting\u2019s region; pythons aren\u2019t indigenous to Arizona, but maybe there\u2019s some Infrastructure in the Phoenix reptile house. These things happen.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Think about how your character relates to the other PCs. Why would they associate with you? You\u2019re kind of a Cover threat, and one can get into serious legal trouble for harboring certain animals without the proper papers (Ever try to bring a horse into New York City?). Then again, you can sniff out Aether. Particularly innocuous animals make excellent spies. Sleeper agents in Vancouver aren\u2019t usually trained to deal with grizzly bears or king cobras.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Step 2: Assign traits</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">The default assumption here is that you are playing a Cryptid who has attained human-level intelligence, and functions in a human-enough way to empathize with and make a compelling player character. As such, standard character creation rules apply. You start with one dot in each Attribute, and prioritize 5/4/3, keeping in mind your concept, species, background, etc. Most animals prioritize physical Attributes, but then again, maybe your brush with the God Machine changed you in some pretty fundamental ways. It\u2019s your Cryptid, so it\u2019s your call. You can\u2019t raise Attributes above 5 at this point.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Assign Skills in the same way, prioritizing 11/7/4, choosing three Specialties, you know the drill. Many Cryptids have a Specialty relating to their own kind in Animal Ken or the like, but not necessarily.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Assign 10 dots\u2019 worth of Merits. The majority of these are probably going into physical Merits, but not necessarily! Maybe your elephant was weakened by exposure to angelic radiation, and no longer has a Strong Back. Social Merits are tricky for Cryptids; most don\u2019t have many, if any. There was that one clairvoyant octopus who had achieved worldwide fame for a few months, but thankfully the God Machine was able to contain the situation with a cultist slipping some poisoned crab meat into the tank before things got too out of hand.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">You can trade in three Merit dots for an extra dot in Rank.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Step 3: Add the Cryptid template</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Ah, the fun part. First off, determine your starting size and any advantages you may have for your particular species. There\u2019s a list of GMC-compliant animal stats on the Onyx Path blog; for anything else, work with the Storyteller and use common sense. As an animal, you determine your defense with the higher of Dexterity or Wits (plus Athletics).</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">All Cryptids have the Aether Sense, Otherworldly Perception, and Camera Shy advantages, as per page 228 of </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Demon: The Descent</span></i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">. Additionally, it can be assumed that the human-level intelligence of PC Cryptids comes with the ability to understand at least one human language (actually communicating, though, might be tricky). </span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Your Rank determines your Supernatural Tolerance, as well as anything else that might be determined by such a stat (such as range for Aetheric Sense). It starts at one, and can be increased with Merit dots at character creation.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Once you have your Rank, multiply it by three. These are your pool of Evolution Points, which you can spend now. You can use them for the following:</span></span></span><ul><li>1 Evolution Point increases an Attribute by 1. You can increase Attributes above 5 this way, though your maximum score in any Attribute is capped at 5 + Rank, and you can only have up to your Rank in Attributes above 5. So, at Rank 3, you can have up the three different Attributes above 5, each capping at 8.</li> <li>1 Evolution Point grants you two dots in Skills. Unlike Attributes, you can\u2019t raise Skills above 5 at character creation.</li> <li>1 Evolution Point can also give you one dot in a Skill, plus a Specialty in the same Skill.</li> <li>1 Evolution Point will buy any two-dot, internal Merit, or increase an existing internal Merit by one dot. By internal I mean any Merit that measures a personal quality, and not one that relies on external infrastructure. Sleight of Hand, Iron Will, and Danger Sense are examples of internal Merits, while Library, Resources, and Professional Training are external.</li> <li>1 Evolution Point buys a point of Size. Adjust Speed and Health to match.</li> <li>You use Evolution Points to buy Adaptations. Most cost between 1 and 3 Evolution Points (Storyteller\u2019s call). Like the book says, Numina and demonic form powers can work as Adaptations, if everyone\u2019s on board.</li> </ul><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Step 4: The spark of life</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">It\u2019s time to put the finishing touches on your Cryptid. How were you touched by the God Machine? What is your demeanor? How have you changed in appearance and personality since becoming a Cryptid? How have you reacted to this whole \u201crational thought\u201d business? If you have any external Merits, how did you acquire them? Is your Library an e-reader you filched from some unlucky grad student? Did you use your newfound mental powers to hypnotize your zookeeper into a Retainer?</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Consider an animal\u2019s perspective. How might the world appear differently if you can only see in black &amp; white, or rely on your acute olfactory senses to gather information? How do you feel about your now \u201clesser\u201d kin? Social animals that travel in packs or mate for life might have a completely different outlook from more solitary species. Also consider what special needs an animal of your species has. Unless you have an Adaptation for it, deer need grass, sharks need water, lions need antelope.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Choose three Aspirations for your Cryptid\u2014two short-term, and one long-term. Hey, you have aspirations now! You want for more than just pure survival! How lucky you are, to suffer in the existential ways of humanity, without any of the advantages their society brings them!</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Do Cryptids have Ciphers? That would be kind of strange if they did, huh? But then again, Cryptids are pretty strange. Maybe the God Machine has a special plan for you, and so you have a special Cipher, made up of Adaptations rather than Embeds.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Cryptids don\u2019t have Cover. They don\u2019t exist in a quantum state. They have nothing so metaphysical to hide behind. Certain demonic Conditions, like Hunted or Surveilled, might still apply to them, especially when they\u2019re working against the Machine. They don\u2019t exactly have Integrity either; that\u2019s something for the hairless monkeys. Instead, in particularly intense scenes where emotions run high, the Storyteller might have you roll Resolve + Composure. On a success, you keep your cool, though you can choose to take an emotional Condition of the Storyteller\u2019s choice (such as Spooked) in exchange for a Beat. A failure means you revert back to a more animalistic mindset, gaining the Feral Condition.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">--</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Feral</span></b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">You\u2019ve reverted back to more primal cognitive mechanisms, hinging on the fight-or-flight response. As a result, you can\u2019t use any mental Skills. In addition, your ability to understand language is heavily compromised, so any social Skills that involve verbal communication, complexity, or nuance are also off-limits.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Resolution:</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> You eliminate or escape any threats to your immediate well-being and spend a scene in a safe place, your appetites sated.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Beat:</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> n/a</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">--</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Odds &amp; Ends</span></b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Despite their transformation, Cryptids are, at heart, still animals. They can gain the Bonded Condition under the right circumstances. Characters interacting with them can always choose to roll Animal Ken in place of another social Skill. Powers that target animals, such as the Animalism Discipline, or the Contracts of Fang &amp; Talon, can affect them, though they add their Rank as a resistance trait to such meddling.<br /> <br /> I feel like these rules should be tested out in a shorter story before using them in a longer chronicle, as I don't know yet how playing as a Cryptid measures up to more PC-friendly denizens of the World of Darkness. It definitely requires a good amount of Storyteller adjudication to work effectively, but I think it's definitely worth a shot!</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">New Merit: Cryptid Companion (\u25cf - \u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf)</span></b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">You\u2019ve bonded with a Cryptid, one of those unfortunate beasts warped by the God Machine\u2019s touch. It\u2019s loyal to you, though sometimes calling on its aid is more trouble than it\u2019s worth. This Merit is sometimes found among Unchained who master the Hellhound Exploit, cementing their creation\u2019s existence in reality to keep their new pet from withering away.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Prerequisites:</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> Ability to store Aether, Animal Ken 3, or Animal Ken 2 with a relevant Specialty.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">You have a Cryptid companion with a Rank equal to your dots in this Merit. The Storyteller designs the Cryptid, but you are of course allowed input. Your companion has the Bonded Condition with you.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Drawbacks:</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> Your companion probably relies on you for its Aether fix. If you let it go hungry, it\u2019ll go off hunting for food, which could be trouble. In addition, being bonded with a Cryptid means it\u2019s traceable back to you. If anyone witnesses your Cryptid doing anything particularly bizarre (which for higher-Rank Cryptids might include merely existing), you must immediately check for Compromise.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">New Embed: Domesticate (Instrumental)</span></b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Dogs may seem to love their owners, but the affection and loyalty they feel is not exactly in line with human terms; it\u2019s from millennia of domestication and cohabitation, resulting in a sort of symbiotic relationship. The potential for such domestication lurks in the deep corners of nearly every animal\u2019s DNA, and demons sometimes exploit this potential to their advantage.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">This Embed works best against mammals and birds, as their brains most accept domestic conditioning. Against terrestrial reptiles and amphibians, this Embed suffers a -2 penalty. Against ocean-dwelling reptiles and amphibians, and bony/cartilaginous fish, this Embed suffers a -3 penalty. Against invertebrates, this Embed suffers a -4 penalty. However, the Embed does gain a +1 bonus if used on a species that is already domesticated (dogs, sheep, and the like).</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Dice Pool:</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> Wits + Animal Ken</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Action:</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> Instant</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Roll Results</span></b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Dramatic Failure:</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> Whatever the animal was doing or feeling, it is now hostile toward the demon, treating him as an immediate threat. Prey species will often flee the scene, but any animal will attack when cornered.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Failure:</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> No effect; the animal remains undomesticated.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Success:</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> The demon manipulates a quirk in the animal\u2019s DNA, causing it to act as if it had evolved to be domesticated. For a scene, it gains the Bonded Condition with the demon, acting as a loyal pet.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Exceptional Success:</span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> The demon may, in addition, choose to spend a </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i><span style=\"font-size:15px\">dot</span></i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> of Willpower to make the domestication permanent. Until the animal dies, it is Bonded with the demon. This bond does not necessarily carry over if the demon changes Covers, but a simple success on this Embed will adjust the bond to the new identity. </span></span></span><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1423-espritdecalmar","name":"espritdecalmar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1423_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-22T00:27:45-07:00","id":204762,"title":"","content":"Isn't it kind of normal for a cryptid to be unable to pass for a regular animal? Big foot, greys, goatmen, rats the size of dogs, jersey devils, not saying there aren't cryptids that can pass for regular animals but a lot of the time I'd think they're &quot;call the national guard, or the Inquirer,&quot; not animal control.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-22T05:38:37-07:00","id":204884,"title":"","content":"I got the intuition from the text that it varies. I think the rules are still valid for playing as Bigfoot or the Chupacabra though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1423-espritdecalmar","name":"espritdecalmar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1423_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-10T12:18:50-07:00","id":622520,"title":"","content":"Awesome work, espritdecalmar! I would suggest that you formatted your guide to be more pleasing to the eye. More paragraph breaks, different font sizes, some bolding and perhaps spoiler tags to make it smaller.<br /> <br /> Regarding the observation that God-Machine Cryptids tend to be obviously monstrous, I'd say that you could write up an Adaptation that mimics abilities like urban camouflaging and weird brain glands that control the path of nearby photons.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Bye.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4443-gibberingeloquence","name":"GibberingEloquence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4443_5.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T21:07:40-07:00","id":623909,"title":"","content":"Ah, I'm glad you think so, pun-cat. (Side question: What exactly is going on in your avatar? Is a cat really being fed spaghetti? Can felines even eat wheat and wheat by-products? Is this animal cruelty? When is meatsleep going to release a new video?) This was a while ago, but I'm pretty sure I copy/pasted all this from a Google Doc, hence the strange formatting.<br /> boo<br /> As for your suggestion: That seems reasonable. To be honest I had kind of forgotten about this; I'd written it around the time I'd come up with a Dungeon World playbook for playing as an animal, all JRPG style, and also probably when I was looking for a skull, but if anyone actually does use these rules, I'd love to hear about your experiences.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1423-espritdecalmar","name":"espritdecalmar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1423_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T21:35:39-07:00","id":623918,"title":"","content":"Hey that's pretty cool. I liked the idea of playing cryptids so much I did a one shot between stories where my players played a group of cryptids.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1064-miss_minty","name":"miss_minty","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"283"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}